---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import DiffRow from '../../components/DiffRow.astro';
import FrancoDemo from '../../components/FrancoDemo.astro';
import ErrorDescent from '../../components/ErrorDescent.astro';

// TODO(moaz): check every number on this page against the results table in
// the franco-arabic-translator README before publishing.
const results = [
  { version: 'Rules, longest match', cer: '0.399', exact: '0%' },
  { version: 'Seq2seq from scratch, 1M pairs', cer: '0.259', exact: '7.0%' },
  { version: '+ spelling-variant augmentation', cer: '0.247', exact: '9.0%' },
  { version: 'ByT5 fine-tuned (300M parameters)', cer: '0.299', exact: '10.8%' },
  { version: '+ about 1% real, hand-translated chat', cer: '0.223', exact: '12.6%', best: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Franco: what moved the error rate | Moaz Elkhashab"
  description="A Franco-Arabic to Egyptian Arabic translator: six changes, each scored on a hand-built test set of real messages."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="head grid">
      <!-- TODO(moaz): rewrite the title and lead in your own voice. -->
      <h1>Franco: what actually moved the error rate</h1>
      <p class="lead col-text">
        Franco translates between Franco-Arabic (Arabic typed in Latin letters and digits) and Egyptian Arabic. I
        scored every change on the same hand-built test set of 445 real messages and kept only what lowered the
        character error rate. Here are the changes, in order.
      </p>
      <p class="col-note">
        CER is the character-level edit distance divided by the length of the reference. Lower is better.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="demo-top"><FrancoDemo /></div>

    <div class="wrap-in">
      <p class="lead2">
        The translator runs offline in a public Android app: character error rate 0.207 from Franco to Arabic and 0.249
        from Arabic to Franco.
      </p>
      <div class="demo-diff">
        <DiffRow
          animate
          source="ezayak ya sa7by"
          a={{ label: 'first model', text: 'ايا ي صاحبي' }}
          b={{ label: 'final model', text: 'ازيك يا صاحبي' }}
        >
          <figcaption>
            Both rows are outputs of my model for a sentence I typed by hand. Teal marks where the rows agree, coral
            where they differ. About 1% real, hand-translated chat fixed it.
          </figcaption>
        </DiffRow>
      </div>
      <!-- TODO(moaz): run the first model on "ezayak ya sa7by" and confirm the first-model row is its real output. -->
    </div>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="results">
      <div class="col-wide">
        <h2 id="results">Results on the 445-message test set</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Version</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">CER</th>
                <th scope="col" class="num">Exact match</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {results.map((r) => (
                <tr class:list={{ best: r.best }}>
                  <td>{r.version}</td>
                  <td class="num">{r.cer}</td>
                  <td class="num">{r.exact}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="meta tnote">
          Hamza variants are folded to a bare alef in training and scoring, and this is declared in the repo README.
          Emoji in the references are counted as errors the model cannot avoid.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <ErrorDescent />

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="s1">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="s1">1. Rules hit a ceiling</h2>
        <p>
          I started with a rule-based transliterator built from my own mapping table. Its CER was about 0.40. Every
          rule I edited moved the errors somewhere else: turning a word-final “a” into <bdi lang="ar">ه</bdi> fixed
          “keda” and broke “noura”.
        </p>
        <p>
          Franco has no spelling rules. “7abibi” and “habibi” are both <bdi lang="ar">حبيبي</bdi>. A lookup table
          cannot know which one the writer meant, so the context has to be learned.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="s2">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="s2">2. More data from the same source did not help</h2>
        <p>
          I trained a character-level sequence-to-sequence model (bidirectional GRU encoder, Bahdanau attention, 1.7M
          parameters) on a corpus generated by an LLM. Going from 195K to 1M training pairs improved the validation
          loss, but not the real test: like for like, the CER moved from 0.238 to 0.247.
        </p>
        <p>The model had become better at its own training distribution, not at real chat.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="s3">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="s3">3. The corpus was too consistent</h2>
        <p>
          I counted spellings. In the training corpus “el” outnumbered “al” 99 to 1. In real messages it was 2.4 to
          1. The LLM used one spelling per word; people do not.
        </p>
        <p>
          I added spelling-variant augmentation to the training split only, with substitution rates taken from the
          measured gap. CER went from 0.259 to 0.247 and exact match from 7.0% to 9.0%.
        </p>
        <p>
          Validation loss got worse (2.438 to 2.558) while the real CER got better. The clean validation set
          punished exactly the variants that real messages need.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="col-note">Judging by validation loss alone, I would have rejected the best change so far.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="s4">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="s4">4. A model 175 times bigger did not win</h2>
        <p>
          I fine-tuned ByT5 (300M parameters). It fixed “ezayak ya sa7by”, which my model could not, but its overall
          CER was 0.299. It rewrote foreign-language rows, produced garbage for emoji, and looped on stretched words
          like “3ayzaaaak”.
        </p>
        <p>
          The two models failed on different rows. An oracle that always picked the better output would have reached
          0.228, but a simple router did not get near that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="s5">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="s5">5. About 1% real data worked</h2>
        <p>
          I built 9,849 pairs from real chats: personal details removed and every translation reviewed by me. Repeated
          five times in training, they were about 1% of the corpus before repetition. CER went from 0.247 to 0.223
          and exact match to 12.6%.
        </p>
        <p>The sentence that failed all project long now works in the small model:</p>
      </div>
      <div class="col-wide fig">
        <DiffRow
          source="ezayak ya sa7by"
          a={{ label: 'first model', text: 'ايا ي صاحبي' }}
          b={{ label: 'final model', text: 'ازيك يا صاحبي' }}
        >
          <figcaption>A sentence I typed by hand. Teal marks agreement between the rows, raspberry marks differences.</figcaption>
        </DiffRow>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="s6">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="s6">6. A harder test, and the production model</h2>
        <p>
          I collected chats from 40+ more writers and split by sender, so nobody appears in both training and test
          (19.4K training pairs, 1,925 test pairs). I standardised spelling in both sets, added learning-rate decay
          and trained again. On the harder test, CER went from 0.273 for the previous model to 0.207, and exact match
          from 16% to about 29%.
        </p>
        <p>
          These numbers are on a different test set with a different scoring convention, so they do not belong in the
          table above.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="s7">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="s7">7. Shipping it</h2>
        <p>
          I exported the model to ONNX with decoding inside the graph: about 37 ms per translation, fully offline.
          The Dart tokenizer is unit-tested token for token against the Python one. The Flutter app went through
          closed testing and was released publicly on Google Play in October 2026.
        </p>
        <!-- TODO(moaz): confirm the final Arabic to Franco CER (the CV says 0.249) and add one sentence on it. -->
        <ul class="links">
          <li><a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.moazreda.franco_translator">Google Play</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/MoazReda/franco-translator-app">App code</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/MoazReda/franco-arabic-translator">Model code</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .wrap-in { max-width: 1120px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 8px 20px 0; }
  .lead2 { font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.5; max-width: 40ch; margin: 24px 0 28px; }
  .demo-diff { max-width: 780px; }
  .head {
    padding-top: 40px;
    row-gap: 24px;
  }
  .head h1 {
    grid-column: 1 / span 10;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6.5vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .tnote {
    margin-top: 12px;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .demo-top {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  .fig {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .head h1 {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }
</style>
